# ==============================================================================
# .env.local — customer secrets (GITIGNORED, never commit)
# ==============================================================================
#
# This template is what `/project-init` writes to .env.local for you.
# Edit by hand if you need to add more env profiles or change individual values.
#
# config.js (the layered env loader) reads, in order:
#   1. .env.defaults          — plugin-supplied constants (sandbox cards, etc.)
#   2. .env.${TEST_ENV}       — customer-required per-env values (URLs, IDs)
#   3. .env.local             — THIS FILE: what is the same for every env (the
#                               tracker / code-host connection) + secrets with
#                               per-env suffix promotion
#   4. test-accounts.local.json — every TEST ACCOUNT's login and password, per
#                               env, plus the master password (gitignored). It
#                               beats every .env* file. Test accounts are NOT kept
#                               here any more; a project that still has them here
#                               keeps working until /project-init --check moves them.
#
# Per-env suffix promotion: when TEST_ENV=qa, the loader auto-promotes any var
# ending in `_QA` to its base name. So `SOME_SETTING_QA=…` becomes
# `SOME_SETTING` at runtime.
#
# Rules:
#   - Env suffix must match [A-Z0-9_]+ (uppercase of your TEST_ENV).
#   - TEST_ENV must match [a-z0-9_]+ — kebab-case is rejected.
# ==============================================================================

# --- Project settings — the same for every environment -------------------------
# The tracker / code-host connection. One value for the whole project, so it is
# kept here once rather than in every .env.<env>. Identifiers, not secrets: the
# API tokens live in vc-secrets. Uncomment only the lines for your tracker / host
# and fill them in: this file loads LAST, so an uncommented line — even an empty
# one — overrides the same key in .env.<env>. No default ships here on purpose.
# JIRA_EMAIL is the account the Jira API token belongs to — Jira Cloud accepts a
# token only together with it.
# Jira:
# JIRA_BASE_URL=
# JIRA_PROJECT_KEY=
# JIRA_EMAIL=
# Azure Boards / Azure Repos:
# ADO_ORG=
# ADO_PROJECT=
# GitHub-hosted client repos:
# CLIENT_REPO_ORG=

# --- Test accounts — NOT here -------------------------------------------------
# Every test account's login and password (USER_EMAIL / USER_PASSWORD, ADMIN /
# ADMIN_PASSWORD, USER2_*, MULTI_ORG_USER_*, the seeding passwords such as
# B2B_USER_PASSWORD / DEFAULT_TEST_PASSWORD, the loyalty and agent-slot accounts,
# MASTER_PASSWORD) lives in test-accounts.local.json in the project root,
# per env under envs.<env>. A browser lane types the bare NAME for its env or
# NAME_<ENV> (both work; MASTER_PASSWORD on a master stand), never the value.
# No literal password ships in this template.

# --- API + Integration secrets (cross-env, usually one set per customer) -----
# These don't typically vary per env (one API key per provider account).
FIGMA_API_KEY=
POSTMAN_API_KEY=
BUILDER_IO_EMAIL=
BUILDER_IO_PASSWORD=

# --- Microsoft Teams (optional — /qa-monitoring Teams card) ------------------
# Incoming webhook URL for the channel that should receive the monitoring
# summary card (skills/qa-monitoring/notify-teams.ts). Leave blank to skip —
# /qa-monitoring still runs and writes its report without it.
TEAMS_WEBHOOK_URL=

# --- Atlassian / JIRA (for /qa-bug filing, /qa-status, /qa-test-plan) --------
# Typically configured at MCP level, not env vars. See docs/onboarding.md
# § Atlassian MCP setup.
# JIRA_PROJECT_KEY itself is per-env (lives in .env.${TEST_ENV}).

# --- BrowserStack (optional — only if you run cross-browser cloud tests) -----
BROWSERSTACK_USERNAME=
BROWSERSTACK_ACCESS_KEY=
